<article class="env-remark" id="0BP9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BP9">Remark <span data-tag="0BP9">15.92.17</span>.</a> Let $(R \to R', f)$ be a glueing pair. Let $M$ be an $R$-module that is not necessarily glueable for $(R \to R', f)$. Setting $M' = M \otimes _ R R'$ and $M_1 = M_ f$ we obtain the glueing datum $\text{Can}(M) = (M', M_1, \text{can})$. Then $\tilde M = H^0(M', M_1, \text{can})$ is an $R$-module that is glueable for $(R \to R', f)$ and the canonical map $M \to \tilde M$ gives isomorphisms $M \otimes _ R R' \to \tilde M \otimes _ R R'$ and $M_ f \to \tilde M_ f$, see Theorem <a href="/tag/0BP2" data-tag="0BP2">15.92.16</a>. From the exactness of the sequences </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  M \to (M \otimes _ R R' )\oplus M_ f \to M \otimes _ R (R')_ f \to 0  \]
</div>
  <p> and </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to \tilde M \to (\tilde M \otimes _ R R') \oplus \tilde M_ f \to \tilde M \otimes _ R (R')_ f \to 0  \]
</div>
  <p> we conclude that the map $M \to \tilde M$ is surjective. </p>
</article>